  "dom_id": "step-q134033",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/24d3507598aa125a515534fd79c82e051875e897bedbe333cdaaf4e4856f67e4.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/f62fe2cf8d93e0737482334a6f659202032160d451e6518afa152f43fb51d0a7.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})\u2248(2,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})\u2248(4,2).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & \u2248\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-6}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 6 & =-2(2)+b \\\\ 6 & =-4+b \\\\ 10 & =b \\\\ b & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-2x+10.\n$$",
